WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.626 --> 00:00:02.961
Narrator: Dentures.

00:00:02.961 --> 00:00:05.463
They're often used as comic relief

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:08.341
in movies and Pixar animated shorts,

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:15.181
but they play a big
part in the daily lives

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:18.601
of about 20 million people in America,

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:21.813
and it's not just older
people wearing them.

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:23.940
But how exactly are they made?

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.779
Dentures have come a long way.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:32.866
This set took two fittings and was shipped

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:36.911
between two locations
before it was ready to wear.

00:00:36.911 --> 00:00:40.040
The first thing to know is
that a full set of dentures

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:44.669
has a flesh-colored acrylic
base that fits over the gums.

00:00:44.669 --> 00:00:47.797
The upper portion covers
the roof of your mouth,

00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:49.799
and the lower is a horseshoe shape

00:00:49.799 --> 00:00:52.052
that accommodates the tongue.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.262
Now that we know the basics,

00:00:54.262 --> 00:00:56.639
let's learn how dentures are made.

00:00:56.973 --> 00:01:00.351
First, a prosthodontist 
makes an impression

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:03.563
to capture the patient's
upper and lower gums.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:07.484
A thick paste gets placed
into the patient's mouth

00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:09.736
for a few seconds.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:13.031
While the impression is still gooey,

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:17.452
it's poured into a stone,
which acts as a cast.

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:21.331
This cast is now a replica
of the patient's mouth,

00:01:21.331 --> 00:01:24.209
which comes in handy
during the final stages

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:25.919
of creating the dentures.

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:28.296
In the meantime,

00:01:28.296 --> 00:01:31.257
the patient tries on
different sets of dentures

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.218
to determine the size and color

00:01:33.218 --> 00:01:35.220
of their soon-to-be teeth.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.266
From there, the prosthodontist 
makes a few adjustments to the cast.

00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:45.522
Next, the cast of the
mouth is sent to the lab

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:47.482
where the dentures get made.

00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:50.902
Using the patient's cast as reference,

00:01:50.902 --> 00:01:54.948
the lab starts to create the
wax version of the gum line.

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:59.452
The teeth, made of acrylic resin —

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:02.747
a material that looks very
similar to real teeth —

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:04.624
are set in place.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:08.253
Minor touches get made to
the wax around the teeth

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:11.631
then get sent back to the prosthodontist.

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:14.384
After the wax dentures are approved

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:18.054
by both the patient and the
prosthodontist for fit and look,

00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:21.683
they're sent back to the
lab to finish the process.

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:24.352
The wax is just temporary.

00:02:24.352 --> 00:02:26.646
It gets replaced by an acrylic,

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:28.690
which is more aesthetically pleasing

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.066
and realistic-looking.

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:35.822
The lab boils the dentures to
get rid of the wax portions.

00:02:41.119 --> 00:02:44.038
Holes are indented into the teeth

00:02:44.038 --> 00:02:46.249
so the material can attach to it.

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:51.462
Then, a machine called the IvoBase

00:02:51.462 --> 00:02:54.257
injects the acrylic liquid into the molds

00:02:54.257 --> 00:02:56.092
to stick to the denture teeth.

00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:07.145
The dentures are cleaned up and polished.

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:15.904
They're now ready for the patient.

